# Teach the trend guard to see finding turnover (issue #33)

## Context

`assessConvergence` (`src/run.ts:483`) has two arms. The **repeat arm** (`src/run.ts:489-499`) compares `p1Signature` — a hash of the sorted P0/P1 ids — and stops when the identical set comes back `oscillationThreshold` rounds running. The **trend arm** (`src/run.ts:501-527`) reads only `RoundRecord.count` and stops when no round in the window has a strictly lower count than its predecessor.

The trend arm ignores the ids that `recordRound` (`src/run.ts:378`) already stores in the same record. That stopped the planning run for issue #2 at plan round 4 of 5 — `the P1 count has not fallen in 3 rounds (2 -> 2 -> 2)` — on a history whose three rounds had **zero id overlap with each other**, each round a narrower restatement of the last. Round 4, run by hand, fell to 1. The run was converging the whole time.

Intended outcome: a flat count whose findings turned over completely is no longer read as deadlock, while every genuinely stuck shape stops exactly as it does today.

## The rule

Within the trend window, a flat count is excused — the run continues — only when **both** hold:

1. **Total turnover.** No finding id appears in more than one round of the window. Deliberately over the whole window, not between consecutive rounds: an `{a,b} -> {c,d} -> {a,b}` oscillation has no consecutive overlap and *is* stuck, and it fails this test because `a` occurs twice in any three-round window.
2. **The flat run is exactly one window long.** The trailing run of rounds sharing the latest round's count is exactly `window` rounds — i.e. the round before the window had a different count, or the window is the whole history.

Condition 2 is a deviation from the brief's single-sentence rule, and it is **required** by the hard constraint that no existing test changes. `tests/convergence.test.ts:144` (`a late count that will not fall still stops the run`) is eight rounds flat at 2 with sixteen pairwise-distinct ids: condition 1 on its own would stop that test stopping the run, and the test would have to be edited.

Condition 2 also carries the argument on its own terms: **turnover buys a run one window, not the whole cap.** It is computed from `RoundRecord.count`, adds no config key, and makes the loosening bounded and deterministic — the excuse can fire at most once per run of equal counts.

Condition 2 is also what keeps case 1 (`1 -> 1 -> 3`) stopping: a rising last round makes the trailing flat run 1, not 3, so the excuse never engages and today's verdict is untouched.

### Which ids the check reads

The stored blocker ids, exactly as `RoundRecord.ids` holds them — no change to what `guardProgress` records. `guardProgress` (`src/orchestrator.ts:911-916`) passes `p1Signature(all)` for the signature and `blockers.map((f) => f.id)` for the ids, but this is not a mismatch worth correcting here: `p1Signature` filters to P0/P1 (`src/run.ts:335-344`) and the `blockers` helper in `src/validate.ts` selects the same severities, so non-blocking findings are already excluded from both. Widening `recordRound`'s ids to all findings would make turnover *and* `persistenceNotice` inspect P2/P3 items and would change existing semantics on the reporting path. The trend arm is a statement about the **blocking** count, so blocking ids are the matching population.

### What still stops, and why

| Case | Stopped by |
| --- | --- |
| `1 -> 1 -> 3`, all ids distinct | flat run is 1 ≠ window → trend arm, unchanged |
| Flat count, one id surviving the window | that id occurs 3 times → no turnover |
| `{a,b} -> {c,d} -> {a,b}` and longer cycles | `a`, `b` occur twice → no turnover |
| Identical set 3 rounds running | repeat arm, untouched |
| Records with absent/empty `ids` (legacy `state.json`) | treated as "cannot tell" → no turnover |
| Long flat run with turnover (8 rounds at 2) | flat run 8 ≠ window |
| Verification loop (`id: 'verification-failing'`, `src/orchestrator.ts:1024`) | one stable id that is the whole set → repeat arm |

## File-by-file changes

### 1. `src/run.ts` — two module-private helpers, placed immediately above `assessConvergence`

```ts
/**
 * Whether every finding in the window is new to it - no id in more than one
 * round.
 *
 * Over the whole window rather than between neighbours, so an {a,b} -> {c,d} ->
 * {a,b} oscillation - which has no consecutive overlap and is genuinely stuck -
 * fails it.
 *
 * A round with absent or empty ids means "cannot tell", not "no overlap": a
 * state.json written before RoundRecord.ids existed has none, and reading the
 * rule literally there would be vacuously true and would switch the trend brake
 * off for every resumed legacy run.
 */
function windowTurnedOver(recent: readonly RoundRecord[]): boolean {
  const seen = new Map<string, number>();
  for (const r of recent) {
    const ids = r.ids;
    if (ids === undefined || ids.length === 0) return false;
    // Deduped per round, so a round that repeats an id within itself is not
    // mistaken for the same id surviving across rounds.
    for (const id of new Set(ids)) seen.set(id, (seen.get(id) ?? 0) + 1);
  }
  for (const n of seen.values()) if (n > 1) return false;
  return true;
}

/** Trailing rounds whose blocking count equals the latest round's. */
function flatRun(history: readonly RoundRecord[]): number {
  const target = history[history.length - 1]?.count;
  if (target === undefined) return 0;
  let rounds = 0;
  for (let i = history.length - 1; i >= 0; i -= 1) {
    if (history[i]?.count !== target) break;
    rounds += 1;
  }
  return rounds;
}
```

`persistentStreak` (`src/run.ts:536`) is deliberately **not** reused here, despite the brief's preference: it measures only the trailing streak of an id present in the **last** round, so it reports 1 for `{a,b} -> {c,d} -> {a,b}` and would let a real oscillation through. `windowTurnedOver` subsumes everything `persistentStreak` would have contributed to this decision. `persistentStreak` and `persistenceNotice` are left exactly as they are for the warning path.

### 2. `src/run.ts` — the trend arm of `assessConvergence` (`src/run.ts:518-525`)

Add a guard ahead of the existing return, keeping the message construction byte-for-byte:

```ts
  if (!improved) {
    // A flat count whose findings turned over completely is not deadlock: the
    // planning run for #2 was stopped at 2 -> 2 -> 2 on three rounds with zero
    // id overlap, each a narrower restatement of the last, and the round that
    // followed fell to 1. The repeat arm above already treats identity as
    // meaningful; this is the one place that information was dropped.
    //
    // Bounded on purpose. Turnover buys one window, not the cap: the flat run
    // must have started inside this window, so the excuse fires at most once
    // per run of equal counts. It says nothing about a rising count, which
    // leaves the flat run shorter than the window and stops exactly as before.
    //
    // Loosening a brake is worth being nervous about; the blast radius is one
    // window of rounds the run was already allowed. maxPlanRounds /
    // maxReviewRounds, guardPlanBudget and budget.maxTokens all still bind.
    if (window > 1 && flatRun(history) === window && windowTurnedOver(recent)) return null;

    const trail = recent.map((r) => r.count).join(' -> ');
    const left = cap - round;
    return (
      `the P1 count has not fallen in ${window} rounds (${trail})` +
      (left > 0 ? ` with ${left} round(s) left` : '')
    );
  }
```

The `window > 1` clause exists because `loop.convergenceWindow` is validated only as `>= 1` (`src/config.ts:408-418`): at `window: 1`, `improved` is always false and `flatRun >= 1` always, so a single round with ids would excuse every late round and disable the brake outright.

Nothing else in `assessConvergence` moves: it still takes a history plus `ConvergenceArgs`, returns a reason or null, touches no `RunState`, config or logging, and stays in `run.ts`. `p1Signature`, the repeat arm, `LATE_ROUND_FRACTION`, `TREND_FLOOR`, `late` and `hasWindow` are untouched, so a run that never reaches the trend arm is unaffected.

Both loops get the change for free: `guardProgress` (`src/orchestrator.ts:904`) is the only caller and is shared by `planPhase` (`src/orchestrator.ts:352`), the verification loop (`:415`) and `reviewPhase` (`:549`).

### 3. `tests/convergence.test.ts` — append tests, edit none

Reuse the file's existing `findings`, `historyOf` and `ARGS` helpers and the `STUCK` constant. `cap: 5, round: 4` reproduces the real trigger exactly (`Math.ceil(5 * 0.75) === 4`).

```ts
/** The plan rounds from issue #2, verbatim - three rounds at 2, no id in common. */
const ISSUE_2: readonly (readonly string[])[] = [
  ['schema-aware-claude-test-doubles', 'non-object-roles-bypass-validation'],
  ['no-codex-role-preflight-blocks', 'malformed-role-derivation-before-validation'],
  ['disabled-role-preflight-enforcement', 'role-scoped-agent-resolution-mutates-shared-config'],
];
```

1. `'a flat count with completely new findings each round is not deadlock'` — `assessConvergence(historyOf(...ISSUE_2), { ...ARGS, cap: 5, round: 4 })` is `null`. Also assert the fixture's premise: `new Set(history.flatMap((r) => r.ids ?? [])).size === 6` and `new Set(history.map((r) => r.signature)).size === 3`.
2. `'an alternating blocking set still stops the run'` — `historyOf(['a-1','a-2'], ['c-1','c-2'], ['a-1','a-2'])` at `cap: 5, round: 4` matches `/has not fallen in 3 rounds/`; and the longer cycle `['a-1','a-2'], ['c-1','c-2'], ['a-1','a-2'], ['c-1','c-2']` at `cap: 12, round: 8` likewise.
3. `'a flat count with a surviving core still stops the run'` — `historyOf([STUCK,'n-1'], [STUCK,'n-2'], [STUCK,'n-3'])` at `cap: 5, round: 4` matches `/has not fallen in 3 rounds/`.
4. `'a rising count stops however new the findings are'` — `historyOf(['p-1'], ['q-1'], ['r-1','r-2','r-3'])` at `cap: 5, round: 4` matches `/has not fallen in 3 rounds/` and contains the trail `1 -> 1 -> 3`.
5. `'a history recorded without ids stops exactly as it did before'` — literal records `[{signature:'aaa',count:2},{signature:'bbb',count:2},{signature:'ccc',count:2}]` (distinct signatures, so the repeat arm is provably not what fires) at `cap: 5, round: 4` matches `/has not fallen in 3 rounds/`; repeat the assertion with `ids: []` on each record.
6. `'turnover buys one window, not the run'` — six rounds at 2 with twelve pairwise-distinct ids, `cap: 12, round: 8`, matches `/has not fallen in 3 rounds/`. This pins condition 2 directly and documents why `tests/convergence.test.ts:144` still passes.
7. `'an early round and a partial window are untouched'` — `historyOf(...ISSUE_2).slice(0, 2)` at `cap: 5, round: 2` is `null`; the full `ISSUE_2` history at `cap: 12, round: 3` is `null` (not late).
8. `'turnover does not rescue an identical set'` — `historyOf([STUCK,'g-1'], [STUCK,'g-1'], [STUCK,'g-1'])` at `cap: 5, round: 4` still matches `/same P1 set came back 3 rounds/`.

### 4. `tests/guard-progress.test.ts` — append one test, edit none

`'the issue-2 plan rounds are never escalated'`: replay `ISSUE_2` plus a fourth round `['stored-null-roles-fallback']` through the file's existing `guardedRound(DEFAULTS, history, ids, { cap: 5, round: idx + 1 })`, asserting `thrown === null` on every round. This proves the fix reaches a real loop through `guardProgress`, which both `planPhase` and `reviewPhase` call, and that the recorded ids the guard itself produces (`blockers.map((f) => f.id)`) are what the turnover check reads.

## Order of work

1. Add `windowTurnedOver` and `flatRun` to `src/run.ts`.
2. Change the trend arm. Do 1 and 2 in the same edit — `noUnusedLocals` is on, so unused helpers fail `tsc`.
3. Add the `tests/convergence.test.ts` tests (depends on 1–2).
4. Add the `tests/guard-progress.test.ts` test (depends on 1–2).

## Verification

- `npm run typecheck` — `strict`, `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, `exactOptionalPropertyTypes` and `noUnusedLocals` are on; index access in `flatRun` must go through `?.` and `r.ids` must be narrowed rather than asserted.
- `npm test` (`pretest` runs `npm run build`, then `node --test "dist/tests/**/*.test.js"`). Requirements: **all 407 pre-existing tests pass with no edits**, the new tests pass, and the suite is green 3 consecutive times (vibe runs this itself).
- While iterating: `npm run build && node --test dist/tests/convergence.test.js dist/tests/guard-progress.test.js`.
- `git diff --stat -- tests/` must show additions only; no line removed from an existing test.

## Edge cases and failure modes

- **Legacy state on resume.** `state.json` written before `RoundRecord.ids` existed has records with no ids; `windowTurnedOver` returns false, so the brake behaves exactly as today. Test 5.
- **Duplicate id within one round.** Deduped with `new Set(ids)` before counting, so it cannot masquerade as survival across rounds.
- **Count 0.** `guardProgress` is only reached with blockers outstanding, but a 0-count record carries empty ids and falls to "cannot tell" — it stops as today.
- **`convergenceWindow: 1`.** Excluded explicitly by `window > 1`; without it the excuse would fire on every late round.
- **Verification loop.** Its synthetic finding has the stable id `verification-failing` and is the whole set, so its signature repeats and the repeat arm stops it at `oscillationThreshold` rounds before the trend arm is reached. Covered by the existing test at `tests/guard-progress.test.ts:91`, which must keep passing untouched.
- **Failure mode if the rule is too loose.** A stuck run burns at most one extra window of rounds; `maxPlanRounds`/`maxReviewRounds`, `guardPlanBudget` and `budget.maxTokens` all still bind. That bound is stated in the comment where the rule lives.

## Rollback

One `if` and two pure helpers in `src/run.ts`, plus additive tests. No state format change, no config key, nothing new persisted. Reverting the commit restores today's behaviour exactly; no migration is needed because `RoundRecord` is unchanged.
